title: 'pallet-revive: fix v4 migration double credit and partial swap'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    Fixes `pallet_revive::migrations::v4` for runtimes that switched `Config::Deposit` to
    `PGasDeposit` before scheduling v4.

    Phase 1 now clears the pallet account's `NativeDepositOf` rows and rebuilds them from
    `CodeInfoOf`. Before, it added to rows already written since the switch, crediting those code
    deposits twice. `PGasDeposit::migrate_native_to_pgas` now rolls back if minting or holding
    PGAS fails. Before, phase 2 could burn a contract's native deposit and leave nothing in its
    place, for example when the PGAS asset was not registered yet. Phase 2 now swaps only the part
    of a contract's native hold not recorded in its `NativeDepositOf` rows. Before, it swapped the
    whole hold, so payers who paid native after the switch could no longer free storage.

    `CodeInfoOf` does not record which asset paid a code deposit. When the pallet account already
    holds PGAS under `CodeUploadDepositReserve`, phase 1 no longer rebuilds its rows: it keeps the
    rows written since the switch and swaps the native they do not account for to PGAS, as phase 2
    does for contracts. Code uploaded before the switch is then refunded from PGAS, like code paid
    in PGAS. Before, phase 1 credited PGAS-paid code as native, so its owners were refunded from
    other uploaders' native deposits and the last owner's `remove_code` failed with
    `StorageRefundLocked`.

    Phase 3 now rewrites only `DeletionQueue` slots still in the legacy `TrieId` layout. Before, it
    also rewrote items queued in the new layout after the runtime upgrade, replacing their contract
    account with the zero placeholder. The deletion processor then never cleared that contract's
    `NativeDepositOf` rows, and its address could not be instantiated again
    (`PendingDepositCleanup`).

    A contract, or the pallet account for code deposits, has one `NativeDepositOf` row per payer,
    and nothing bounds how many. Each swap now reads those rows in chunks across steps, keeping the
    last payer and the native summed so far in the cursor, and is charged for the rows it reads
    before reading them. A step can no longer exceed the migration's weight or proof size limit
    because one account has many payers.

    Runtimes that already ran v4 are unaffected. Runtimes that have not:
    - Register the PGAS asset before scheduling v4, and run the `try-runtime` migration checks.
      v4 never fails on chain, since a failed migration is handled by `FailedMigrationHandler`,
      which commonly freezes the chain. Without the asset every swap rolls back, v4 completes with
      nothing converted and is not run again. `pre_upgrade` rejects a missing asset and
      `post_upgrade` rejects any account left on native, so the checks catch this before release.
    - If you implement `pallet_revive::WeightInfo` yourself, add `v4_clear_code_credit_step`, or
      regenerate your weights.
    - `pallet_revive::migrations::v4::Cursor` is now generic over the account id and balance, with
      `CursorOf<T>` as the runtime's alias, and has new `ClearCodeCredits(Option<AccountId>)`,
      `CodeRows` and `ContractRows` variants. Code that names the type should use `CursorOf<T>`;
      code that matches on it must handle the new variants.
crates:
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: major
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch